<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anton Bruckner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1824">1824</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Joseph Anton Bruckner</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anton Bruckner was an Austrian composer and organist, born at Ansfelden near Linz, known for his symphonies and sacred music, including Masses, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/te-deum">Te Deum</a> and motets. He worked as a teacher and organist at Sankt Florian, studied with the theorist Simon Sechter, and in 1868 took over Sechter's post teaching music theory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> Conservatory. His long symphonies, with their rich harmony, strong polyphony and unprepared modulations, are regarded as emblematic of the final stage of Austro-German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>. Critics led by Eduard Hanslick attacked their size and repetition, and he revised many of his works, leaving several versions; he died in Vienna in 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner.jpg" alt="Anton Bruckner" width="330" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Anton_Bruckner_(1868).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bruckner was born in Ansfelden on 4 September 1824, the eldest of the five children who survived of eleven born to the village schoolmaster Anton Bruckner Sr. His father was his first music teacher, and he learned the organ as a child, sometimes practising twelve hours a day. After his father died in 1837 he became a choirboy at the Augustinian monastery of Sankt Florian. He worked as an assistant teacher at Windhaag, where pay was appalling and his superior constantly humiliated him, and a belief in his own inferiority stayed with him all his life. From 1845 he spent ten years at Sankt Florian as teacher and organist, and in 1855 he was accepted as a pupil by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> theorist Simon Sechter.</p><p>In 1868 he hesitantly took over Sechter's post at the Vienna Conservatory and concentrated on symphonies, which were poorly received and at times called wild and nonsensical. He was unhappy in Vienna, where the critic Eduard Hanslick dominated musical life; by aligning himself with Wagner in the feud between the followers of Wagner and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms">Johannes Brahms</a>, he made an unintentional enemy of him. Supporters such as the conductors Arthur Nikisch and Franz Schalk brought his music to the public.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Bruckner composed eleven symphonies, from the Study Symphony in F minor of 1863 to the unfinished Symphony No. 9 in D minor, written between 1887 and 1896. Only the Fourth, the Romantic, had a subtitle from the composer. By the mid-twentieth century musicologists recognised that his orchestration followed the sound of the pipe organ, alternating between groups of instruments as an organist changes manual. His sacred music includes a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/te-deum">Te Deum</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnificat">Magnificat</a>, about forty motets and at least seven Masses. Helgoland of 1893 was his last completed composition, and his String Quintet in F major dates from 1879.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bruckner died on 11 October 1896 and is buried at Sankt Florian. His admirers included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Gustav Mahler</a>. The leaders of Nazi Germany approved of his music, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> consecrated a bust of him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walhalla">Walhalla</a> in Regensburg in 1937. The Brucknerhaus in Linz, which opened in 1974, is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anton Bruckner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…emblem of Austria and its monarchy: Beethoven in a work celebrating the end of the Napoleonic Wars, Rossini, Paganini, Donizetti, Clara Schumann, Smetana and <b>Bruckner</b>, while Bartók used it in his symphonic poem Kossuth (1903) as the sign of the Austrian enemy of the Hungarian Revolution of 1848.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</a></li><li><blockquote>Composers have set it since the Renaissance, among them Monteverdi in his Vespers of 1610, Vivaldi, Johann Sebastian Bach (1723, revised 1733), <b>Bruckner</b>, Rachmaninoff and Arvo Pärt.</blockquote><a href="/songs/magnificat">Magnificat</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Anton Bruckner</b>'s first composition set the hymn's first strophe.</blockquote><a href="/songs/pange-lingua-gloriosi-corporis-mysterium">Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium</a></li><li><blockquote>The hymn was first sung to a Gregorian chant melody, and many composers have set it since: Lully, who was fatally injured while conducting his setting for the court of Louis XIV, Charpentier, Handel, Haydn, Mozart, Berlioz, Verdi, <b>Bruckner</b>, Dvořák and Britten among them.</blockquote><a href="/songs/te-deum">Te Deum</a></li><li><blockquote>…tune for four centuries: Jehan Titelouze (1623), Marc-Antoine Charpentier (five settings), Johann Pachelbel, Nicolas de Grigny, Johann Sebastian Bach (the chorale BWV 370 and the organ preludes BWV 631 and 667), Berlioz, Franck, <b>Bruckner</b>, Gustav Mahler, who set the Latin text in Part I of his Symphony No.</blockquote><a href="/songs/veni-creator-spiritus">Veni Creator Spiritus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ansfelden, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,14.29&amp;year=1870" aria-label="Ansfelden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4882 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anton Bruckner"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5332 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5332 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,14.29&amp;year=1870">What was here in 1870? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
